'''Intersubjectivity''' concerns relations between subjects through which experiences, meanings or aspects of a shared world become mutually intelligible. It appears in phenomenology, social theory and research on interaction.

== Philosophical foundations ==
Husserl's investigations of intersubjectivity address the experience of others, empathy, embodiment and the constitution of a world understood as accessible beyond one's own perspective. This is a phenomenological and philosophical inquiry, not a hypothesis that can be settled simply by measuring agreement. Later sociological and interactional approaches emphasize shared practices, norms and meaning-making.

== Interaction and empirical questions ==
Observable phenomena include joint attention, turn-taking, perspective taking and conversational repair. In a referential communication experiment, participants coordinate descriptions of unfamiliar objects; researchers can measure identification accuracy, correction episodes and changes in descriptions over repeated trials. Such measures operationalize particular aspects of shared understanding rather than directly measure another person's subjective experience.

== Distinctions and limits ==
Common ground concerns information treated as mutually available in interaction; theory of mind studies attribution of mental states; intersubjectivity has broader philosophical questions about relations among subjects and shared worlds. Agreement can arise through conformity or shared error. Public evidence and independent verification remain necessary to evaluate truth. Synchronization among computational agents does not by itself establish intersubjective experience in the phenomenological sense.

